  booktitle={2025 13th International Japan-Africa Conference on Electronics, Communications, and Computations (JAC-ECC)}, 
  title={Multi-Scale Fusion of Sentence- and Character-Level Features for Automated Dyslexia Detection}, 
  year={2025},
  volume={},
  number={},
  pages={162-167},
  abstract={Dyslexia is a common but often overlooked learning difficulty that affects how individuals read and write. Traditional diagnostic methods can be costly, time-consuming, and difficult to access, creating barriers to early identification and support. To address these challenges, this paper introduces a deep learning framework that uses handwriting to automatically detect signs of dyslexia. The proposed model combines two complementary learning paths: DenseNet-121 captures broader sentence-level writing patterns, while MobileNetV3-Small focuses on detailed character-level features. These insights are merged through a weighted fusion strategy to enhance detection accuracy. Tests on both synthetic and real handwriting datasets show that the system achieves strong accuracy and generalization. Designed for efficiency and portability, the framework can be deployed in schools, clinics, or low-resource environments. Overall, this work demonstrates that handwriting can serve as an accessible, noninvasive tool for early dyslexia screening, helping move toward more inclusive and equitable education systems.},
